joining us for more perspective on the  pandemic is dr uzma syed an infectious  disease specialist  and head of the kovid task force at good  samaritan hospital in long island new  york  dr syed good morning i know you have  been in the thick of it  at one point working at three different  hospitals 16 hour shifts  give us some perspective doctor what's  it like inside these hospitals now  good morning elaine um so yeah my  colleagues and i have really been  at the forefront of this pandemic for a  year now and  healthcare workers have been doing this  for a long time and frankly they are  exhausted with the ongoing rising  cases hospitalizations and deaths it  just seems like there's no end in sight  dr syed you say that your peers are  exhausted do you see  any room for improvement that you're  hoping to see in the coming days  or weeks as we continue to fight this  well that's really the message that we  keep getting out to americans is that  you know we each  have a part to play in all of this we're  in this together  the more that people can wear their  masks and socially distance and  just really avoid all those behaviors  that help virus transmission  and especially as everyone is moving  indoors and with the holidays  and with the abundance of travel our  hospitals are already  full of patients and it's really  alarming and you know we still do not  have a cure  that's a medication that's 100 percent  curative of this virus so we want people  to know with over 300 000 people that  have lost their lives  we are doing everything that we can  every day to save lives  you know dr syed i wonder what you would  say to folks who  might be traveling as you think about  your own experience and the experience  of your colleagues who have been on the  front lines  if you could give people advice on what  they might do  when they come home from traveling what  they might do  when they arrive at their destinations  that you think might  mitigate the situation so people like  you  aren't seeing increased cases  absolutely i mean pandemic fatigue is  real we are all feeling it  all of us even in healthcare want our  lives back to normal  more than anyone else for travelers i  would really urge them that a test is  really a snapshot in time  so the best thing to do is to really  quarantine before your departure  and then after your arrival you really  need to quarantine as well because now  you've just had high risk of exposure  so you know having a negative test right  before your travel  does not in fact clear you of infection  because you may be  incubating you may have been exposed  during your travel  so it's really prudent to continue  quarantining after you've arrived at  your destination for  about two weeks doctor parts of england  and south africa are  obviously experiencing this new variant  this new  piece of covid19 if you will that  spreads even more easily than what we've  been talking about for the past  nine months do you see the current  vaccines that we have as being able to  protect against those new variants  there is really good reason to believe  that the vaccines will be effective and  in fact the manufacturers are testing  them because  you know the vaccine produces immunity  in many different ways  so we really have good reason to believe  that the vaccines will be effective  but the most important thing to remember  is that the tools that we have  to fight against transmission of this  virus are still effective against this  variant and those include  wearing a mask socially distancing and  really avoiding  indoor gatherings with people that are  outside of your household  great tips thank you so much dr uzma  saeed we appreciate it
 

